surya: Novatek NT36672C over GENI SE0 SPI, milestones 1-4; PSCI power off

sweettouch=2 selects it. Milestone 1: clocks, pins, SPI, bootloader reset,
chip id. Milestone 2: firmware download with the chip's hardware CRC, as
Xiaomi's nvt_download_firmware_hw_crc. The firmware itself is Xiaomi's and is
not in this tree: the locally built ADT carries it as /surya-touch "firmware".
Milestone 3: finger points from the event buffer; reads are paced by gpio9's
edge latched in TLMM (no CPU interrupt), with polling as fallback.
Milestone 4: the points go to iOS through the sweet touch_report/SweetHID path.
Also, with /surya-touch present: PE_halt_restart through PSCI SYSTEM_OFF and
SYSTEM_RESET, so iOS "Shut Down" powers the phone off instead of spinning.
And surya's wall clock: PM6150 RTC + an offset kept in
/private/var/db/surya-time, written when iOS sets the time and applied once
the root is mounted (the RTC itself is not writable from APPS).

Display state and the small things that follow it (21.10): SweetFramebuffer
calls surya_display_power() (0911). With the display off, a double tap
(two short one-finger taps, 400 ms apart, within 150 px) presses the power
key for iOS (suryadoubletap=0 turns it off). The torch: both PM6150L flash
LEDs at 150 mA in torch mode (Linux leds-qcom-flash.c sequence), switched by
a long volume-down press with the display off (sweet_keys poll, 0910). The
white notification LED on PM6150L LPG channel 0 + TRILED (leds-qcom-lpg.c
PWM mode), driven from SweetBattery's timer: steady while charging, blinking
below 15 % on battery, display off only (suryaledtest=1 blinks it at start).
Torch and LED go dark before power off and restart. surya_db_rw() reads and
writes the small state files in /private/var/db (time, backlight).

Vibration (22.10): the AW8624 on GENI SE1 in I2C mode at 400 kHz (pins
gpio0/1, reset gpio63), started 3 s after the touch init so the USB log is up
first: chip id 0x24, Xiaomi's aw8624_haptic_init with its f0 calibration (a
short buzz), then CONT mode, where the chip drives the motor at resonance from
the back-EMF and needs no waveform file. surya_vib_play() takes the patterns
of SweetBattery's "vibrator" service (0901): pairs of intensity (16.16) and
milliseconds, optionally repeated, played by one thread; intensity scales the
drive level; a pattern nobody stopped ends after 120 s. The SE clock code is
shared with the touch (surya_qup_clocks, one lock for the GCC vote register).
suryavib=0 leaves the chip alone.

Measurements, suryastat=1 (22.10, read-only): 60 s after start the OSM
clock tables and requests of L3 and both clusters, every CPU's real
frequency (PMU cycle counter against the 19.2 MHz counter), copy speeds
(cache, panel), the MDSS SSPP/CTL/INTF registers and the panel's frame
rate, and whether 1 s thread_calls on the absolute and continuous clocks
fire; then every 10 s swaps, repeated swaps, panel copy times, vsync
ticks, panel frames, per-CPU idle share and TSENS temperatures.
CPU clocks, suryafreq=max or suryafreq=S,B,L (22.10): row numbers of the
OSM tables for the small cluster, the big one and L3, written to
DCVS_PERF_STATE_DESIRED as Linux's clk-cpu-osm does, 65 s after start;
at 85 C (TSENS) the bootloader's rows come back until below 70 C. Without
the boot-arg nothing is written.
Memory bandwidth, suryaddr=1 (22.10): 70 s after start the top votes for
the RPMh bus clock managers MC0, ACV and SH0 through sweet_rpmh_bcm_vote()
(0912). Nothing is voted otherwise.
surya_fb2x_reset_cache() (22.10): truncates MobileGestalt's cached screen
class once the root is mounted, for the @2x display mode of 0911.
